# {{DISPLAY}} — Features to triage

Rough, triage-ready feature briefs. Each file is a **starting point for the triage → plan → work pipeline** (ship-feature / ship-fleet), not a finished spec — triage grounds every surface in the codebase and sharpens the acceptance sketch into criteria.

**The rule (also in CLAUDE.md): every new feature request lands here FIRST** as a brief (copy `BRIEF-TEMPLATE.md`), then the pipeline runs on the brief. Never build a feature straight from chat — the brief is what makes the work resumable, reviewable, and visible to the orchestrators.

The template is the format the `intake` skill writes, so a brief you write by hand and a brief intake generates are the same shape. Files are named `<slug>.md`; formal ledger IDs are allocated at triage (a serialized write — don't self-allocate). A brief marked `proposed-by-ai: true` is an idea nobody asked for: delete the file to veto it. Where a claim comes from research rather than the owner's ask, cite the exported report on the `research:` line.

## Native features and what the pipeline can prove

A brief whose `platforms:` line names `mac`, `iphone` or `ipad` sits in one of two lanes, and which one belongs in the brief's assumptions:

- **Logic-only** — pure Swift/Rust logic, fully verifiable off-device (`swift test` / `cargo test`).
- **Device-gated** — needs a real device, a simulator, or signing to fully validate. The pipeline still delivers mocks and tested logic; on-device validation is a tracked follow-up rather than part of the same run.

